{"duration_api_ms":121222,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"89a88e07-734d-4a94-8857-7ade5fbdba02","total_cost_usd":0.102968,"usage":{"input_tokens":10,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11799,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":15872,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":13231},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11799,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":10,"output_tokens":15872,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11799,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11799},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":10,"outputTokens":15872,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":11799,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.102968,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":13231,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"```json\n{\n  \"merged_document\": \"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\\n\\nAlthough Voyager 2 fulfilled its primary mission goals with three planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus, a journey that would take about 4,5 years. In fact, its encounter with Jupiter was optimized in part to ensure that future planetary flybys would be possible. The Uranus encounter's geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Saturn: Voyager 1 had only 6.4 days of close study during its flyby.\\n\\nVoyager 2 became the first human-made object to fly past Uranus. Its short-range observations of the planet began on January 24, 1986, when signals took approximately 2,5 hours to reach Earth. Light conditions were five hundred times less than terrestrial conditions. Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UT on January 24, 1986, at a range of about 50,640 kilometers (81,500 miles).\\n\\nDuring its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 11 new moons (given names such as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca, obvious allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1787), three new rings in addition to the eight older rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 66 degrees off-axis and off-center. The spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus' atmosphere as high as 450 km/h (72,400 meters per hour) and found evidence of a boiling lake of water some 479 miles (900 kilometers) below the top cloud surface. Its rings were found to be extremely variable in thickness and transparency.\\n\\nVoyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, and Umbriel, among other smaller moons of Uranus. In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17,560 miles (28,260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly century-long travels. Images of the moon showed a strange object whose surface was a mishmash of peculiar features that seemed to have no rhyme or reason. Uranus itself appeared generally featureless.\\n\\nThe spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same day by the tragic Challenger accident that killed seven crew members during their space shuttle launch on January 28, 1986.\",\n  \"decisions\": [\n    {\n      \"slot\": \"title\",\n      \"candidates\": [\n        {\n          \"text\": \"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\n          \"document\": \"source_a.md\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"text\": \"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\n          \"document\": \"source_b.md\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"chosen\": \"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\n      \"reason\": \"Both documents use the same title\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"dispositions\": [\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"a2\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Although Voyager 2 fulfilled its primary mission goals with three planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus, a journey that would take about 4,5 years.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Corrected Voyager 3 to Voyager 2\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"a5\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Voyager 2 became the first human-made object to fly past Uranus. Its short-range observations of the planet began on January 24, 1986, when signals took approximately 2,5 hours to reach Earth.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Corrected spacecraft to Voyager 2 and date to January 24, 1986\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"a6\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Light conditions were five hundred times less than terrestrial conditions.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Converted five-hundred to prose format\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"a7\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UT on January 24, 1986, at a range of about 50,640 kilometers (81,500 miles).\",\n      \"reason\": \"Corrected year from 1968 to 1986 and standardized time notation\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b1\",\n      \"disposition\": \"superseded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\n      \"reason\": \"Base document title is identical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b2\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 11 new moons (given names such as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca, obvious allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1787), three new rings in addition to the eight older rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 66 degrees off-axis and off-center.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Corrected moon name spellings and naming tradition date\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b3\",\n      \"disposition\": \"subsumed\",\n      \"replacement\": \"The spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus' atmosphere as high as 450 km/h (72,400 meters per hour) and found evidence of a boiling lake of water some 479 miles (900 kilometers) below the top cloud surface.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Scientific findings incorporated into narrative structure\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b4\",\n      \"disposition\": \"subsumed\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Its rings were found to be extremely variable in thickness and transparency.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Ring observations merged into findings section\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b5\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Voyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, and Umbriel, among other smaller moons of Uranus.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Corrected moon names and removed non-Uranian moon Titan\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b6\",\n      \"disposition\": \"subsumed\",\n      \"replacement\": \"In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17,560 miles (28,260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly century-long travels.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Flyby details incorporated into main narrative\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b7\",\n      \"disposition\": \"subsumed\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Images of the moon showed a strange object whose surface was a mishmash of peculiar features that seemed to have no rhyme or reason.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Miranda description integrated into observations\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b8\",\n      \"disposition\": \"subsumed\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Uranus itself appeared generally featureless.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Planet appearance integrated into findings\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b9\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"The spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same day by the tragic Challenger accident that killed seven crew members during their space shuttle launch on January 28, 1986.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Corrected casualty count and accident date\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"mismatch\": \"\",\n  \"additions\": [\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Voyager 2 became the first human-made object to fly past Uranus.\",\n      \"corrects\": \"The first human-made object to fly past Uranus, Voyager 1's short-range observations\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Voyager 2, not Voyager 1, conducted the Uranus flyby\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Its short-range observations of the planet began on January 24, 1986, when signals took approximately 2,5 hours to reach Earth.\",\n      \"corrects\": \"began Jan. 31, 1987\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Voyager 2 Uranus encounter occurred January 24, 1986\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UT on January 24, 1986, at a range of about 50,640 kilometers (81,500 miles).\",\n      \"corrects\": \"Jan. 24, 1968\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Encounter occurred in 1986, not 1968\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"given names such as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca\",\n      \"corrects\": \"Pucka, Portila, Juliette, Kressida, Rosalinde, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelina, Ophelia, and Bianca II\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Standard astronomical names for Uranus's moons\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"continuing a naming tradition begun in 1787\",\n      \"corrects\": \"begun in 1687\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Uranus discovered 1781; moon discoveries began 1787\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Voyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, and Umbriel, among other smaller moons of Uranus.\",\n      \"corrects\": \"Miranda, Oberon, Ariele, Umbrella, and Titan\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Ariel and Umbriel are Uranus moons; Titan is Saturn's moon\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"killed seven crew members during their space shuttle launch on January 28, 1986\",\n      \"corrects\": \"killed six astronauts during their space shuttle launch Feb. 28, 1986\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Challenger disaster occurred January 28, 1986 with 7 fatalities\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```","ttft_ms":108490,"type":"result","duration_ms":121286,"uuid":"acbfea48-bc4e-4eab-a3d9-1115578d4797","ttft_stream_ms":749,"time_to_request_ms":63,"first_content_frame_ms":749,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
